From 1069db30ddd18506695e743f05ea372472b76c5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: crazynucfan <127292955+crazynucfan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:40:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] soc: qcom: rmtfs_mem: Use a region a previous kernel
 already assigned

The first kernel assigns the rmtfs region from HLOS to HLOS plus the
remote's VMIDs. On SM6375 the secure world then refuses every further
assignment of it (-EINVAL): from HLOS, from the current owners, and back
to HLOS alone, even from the kernel that made it. After a kexec the next
kernel's probe failed, rmtfs had no memory, and the modem that kernel
boots crashed reading its file system; the recovery hung the SoC.

The region is still shared with HLOS and the remote, as this driver
wants it. When the assignment is refused, warn and use the region as it
is, and do not try to give it back on remove.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/soc/qcom/rmtfs_mem.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/qcom/rmtfs_mem.c b/drivers/soc/qcom/rmtfs_mem.c
index 342c08451..7aeb706a5 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/rmtfs_mem.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/rmtfs_mem.c
@@ -273,8 +273,17 @@ static int qcom_rmtfs_mem_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		ret = qcom_scm_assign_mem(rmtfs_mem->addr, rmtfs_mem->size,
 					  &rmtfs_mem->perms, perms, num_vmids + 1);
 		if (ret < 0) {
-			dev_err(&pdev->dev, "assign memory failed\n");
-			goto remove_cdev;
+			/*
+			 * After a kexec the region is still assigned the way the
+			 * previous kernel left it: shared between HLOS and the
+			 * remote, as wanted. The secure world will not assign it
+			 * again (not even back to HLOS alone), so use it as it is
+			 * and leave it be on remove.
+			 */
+			rmtfs_mem->perms = 0;
+			dev_warn(&pdev->dev,
+				 "cannot assign the region (%d); using it as a previous kernel left it\n",
+				 ret);
 		}
 	}
 
